[Thinking]
**Exploring vector options**

I need to figure out whether to convert to a vector of characters or strings. There's some ambiguity here. I think I should provide the user with options, such as using `vector<char>` from string begin/end or possibly `vector<uint8_t>`. Another idea is to create `vector<string>` tokens using `istringstream`. I wonder if I should ask the user for more clarity or just present these concise options straight away.
[/Thinking]

It depends what kind of `std::vector` you want:

## 1) `std::string` → `std::vector<char>` (characters)
```cpp
std::string s = "hello";
std::vector<char> v(s.begin(), s.end());
```

If you want the terminating `'\0'` included:
```cpp
std::vector<char> v(s.c_str(), s.c_str() + s.size() + 1);
```

## 2) `std::string` → `std::vector<unsigned char>` / bytes
```cpp
std::string s = "hello";
std::vector<unsigned char> v(s.begin(), s.end());
```
(or `std::vector<std::uint8_t>` similarly)

## 3) `std::string` → `std::vector<std::string>` (split into tokens)
Example split on whitespace:
```cpp
#include <sstream>

std::string s = "a b  c";
std::istringstream iss(s);
std::vector<std::string> v{std::istream_iterator<std::string>(iss),
                           std::istream_iterator<std::string>()};
```

If you tell me the target vector type (`vector<char>`, `vector<uint8_t>`, `vector<string>`, etc.) and the splitting rules (if any), I can give the exact snippet.

